TI  - Maximin Relative Improvement: Fair Learning as a Bargaining Problem
AU  - Jiwoo Han
AU  - Moulinath Banerjee
AU  - Yuekai Sun
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2026)
AB  - When deploying a single predictor across multiple subpopulations, we propose a fundamentally different approach: interpreting group fairness as a bargaining problem among subpopulations. This game-theoretic perspective reveals that existing robust optimization methods such as minimizing worst-group loss or regret correspond to classical bargaining solutions and embody different fairness principles. We propose relative improvement, the ratio of actual risk reduction to potential reduction from a baseline predictor, which recovers the Kalai–Smorodinsky solution. Unlike absolute-scale methods that may not be comparable when groups have different potential predictability, relative improvement provides axiomatic justification including scale invariance and individual monotonicity. We establish finite-sample convergence guarantees under mild conditions.
